  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"An arithmetic sequence begins 7, 12, 17, 22, … What is the 20th term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An arithmetic sequence begins 7, 12, 17, 22, … What is the 20th term?"}]}]},"e":"The common difference is 5 and u_n = 7 + 5(n − 1), so u_20 = 7 + 5(19) = 102. Adding 5 twenty times (107) counts one step too many.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"nth term of an arithmetic sequence","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"u_n = u_1 + (n − 1)d: the first term is already there, so there are only n − 1 steps.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["102","107","100","97"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, … what is the sum of the first 15 terms?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, … what is the sum of the first 15 terms?"}]}]},"e":"d = 5, so the 15th term is 4 + 14(5) = 74. Then S_15 = 15/2 × (4 + 74) = 7.5 × 78 = 585.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic series","st":"sum of an arithmetic series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"S_n = n/2 (u_1 + u_n). Find the last term first; forgetting the final term is the usual error.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["585","555","600","1 170"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence is 3, 6, 12, 24, … What is its 8th term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence is 3, 6, 12, 24, … What is its 8th term?"}]}]},"e":"r = 2, so u_8 = 3 × 2^7 = 3 × 128 = 384. Using 2^8 would add one extra doubling.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"nth term of a geometric sequence","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"u_n = u_1 r^(n−1). Check the index by counting: the 2nd term uses r^1.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["384","768","192","48"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 80, 40, 20, 10, …?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 80, 40, 20, 10, …?"}]}]},"e":"Each term is half the previous one: 40 ÷ 80 = 0.5, and 20 ÷ 40 = 0.5 confirms it. Subtracting (40) would be the arithmetic difference, not the ratio.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"identifying the common ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A common ratio is found by dividing a term by the one before it, not by subtracting.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["0.5","40, because the terms decrease by 40","2","−0.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sequence is defined by u_1 = 4 and u_{n+1} = 3u_n − 2. What is u_3?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sequence is defined by u_1 = 4 and u_{n+1} = 3u_n − 2. What is u_3?"}]}]},"e":"u_2 = 3(4) − 2 = 10, then u_3 = 3(10) − 2 = 28. Applying the rule twice to u_1 (giving u_2 twice) is the most common slip.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"recurrence relations","st":"iterating a recurrence relation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Work term by term: each application of the rule produces exactly one new term.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["28","10","82","34"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the value of Σ (from k = 1 to 5) of (2k + 1)?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the value of Σ (from k = 1 to 5) of (2k + 1)?"}]}]},"e":"The terms are 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, which total 35. Substituting only the upper limit (2(5) + 1 = 11) gives a single term, not the sum.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"sigma notation","st":"evaluating sigma notation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Sigma means ''add the terms'': list them or use the arithmetic-sum formula.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["35","11","30","36"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"What is the sum to infinity of 12 + 6 + 3 + 1.5 + …?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the sum to infinity of 12 + 6 + 3 + 1.5 + …?"}]}]},"e":"r = 0.5 and |r| < 1, so S∞ = a/(1 − r) = 12/0.5 = 24. The sum is finite even though infinitely many terms are added.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"sum to infinity","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"S∞ = a/(1 − r) only when |r| < 1; check that condition before using the formula.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["24","22.5","It does not exist because the series is infinite","48"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which infinite geometric series converges to a finite sum?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which infinite geometric series converges to a finite sum?"}]}]},"e":"A geometric series converges exactly when |r| < 1. Of the four options only the one with r = 1/3 satisfies that; r = 2, r = 1.5 and r = 1 all diverge.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"convergence condition","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Convergence needs |r| < 1. Test the ratio of consecutive terms before summing infinitely.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["5 + 5/3 + 5/9 + 5/27 + …","1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + …","4 + 6 + 9 + 13.5 + …","7 + 7 + 7 + 7 + …"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"$2 000 is invested at 5% per year compound interest. What is its value after 3 years?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2 000 is invested at 5% per year compound interest. What is its value after 3 years?"}]}]},"e":"2000 × 1.05³ = 2000 × 1.157625 = $2 315.25. Adding 3 × 5% = 15% of $2 000 gives $2 300, which ignores that interest is earned on previous interest.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compound growth multiplies repeatedly: A = P(1 + r)^n. Simple interest adds only P × r × n.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["$2 315.25","$2 300.00","$2 315.00","$3 000.00"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A machine costing $8 000 depreciates by 12% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A machine costing "},{"type":"text","text":"$8 000 depreciates by 12% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?"}]}]},"e":"8000 × 0.88² = 8000 × 0.7744 = $6 195.20. Subtracting 24% of $8 000 ($6 080) is wrong because the second year''s 12% is taken from a smaller value.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"depreciation","st":"depreciation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Depreciation is repeated multiplication by (1 − rate), never repeated subtraction of the same amount.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["$6 195.20","$6 080.00","$7 040.00","$6 272.00"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"Solve the exponential equation 3^x = 81 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solve the exponential equation 3^x = 81 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"81 = 3^4, so x = 4. Dividing 81 by 3 gives 27, which is the value of 3^x for x = 3, not the exponent itself.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Write both sides as powers of the same base, then equate the exponents.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 4","x = 27","x = 3","x = 40.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"Without a calculator, state the value of log base 2 of 32.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Without a calculator, state the value of log base 2 of 32."}]}]},"e":"log₂ 32 asks ''2 raised to what power gives 32?'' Since 32 = 2^5, the answer is 5.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"logarithms","st":"evaluating a logarithm","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A logarithm is an exponent: log_b x = y means b^y = x.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["5","16","2.5","6"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"By writing both sides as powers of 2, solve 4^x = 64 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By writing both sides as powers of 2, solve 4^x = 64 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"4 = 2² and 64 = 2^6, so 2^(2x) = 2^6, giving 2x = 6 and x = 3. Dividing 64 by 4 (giving 16) is the value of 4², not the solution.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Convert both sides to the same base: 4^x = (2²)^x = 2^(2x).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 3","x = 16","x = 1.5","x = 2"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"By factorising, state the solutions of the quadratic equation x² − 5x + 6 = 0.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By factorising, state the solutions of the quadratic equation x² − 5x + 6 = 0."}]}]},"e":"Factorising gives (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3. The pair −2 and −3 multiplies to +6 but sums to −5 only if both signs are negative — which they are not here.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"solving a quadratic by factorising","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"For x² + bx + c find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 2 and x = 3","x = −2 and x = −3","x = 1 and x = 6","x = 5 and x = 6"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"The curve y = 2(x + 1)² + 7 is a parabola. What are the coordinates of its turning point?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The curve y = 2(x + 1)² + 7 is a parabola. What are the coordinates of its turning point?"}]}]},"e":"In completed-square form y = a(x − h)² + k the turning point is (h, k). Here h = −1 (because x + 1 = x − (−1)) and k = 7, so the minimum is at (−1, 7).","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"turning point from completed square","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"y = a(x − h)² + k has its turning point at (h, k); watch the sign inside the bracket.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["(−1, 7)","(1, 7)","(−1, −7)","(2, 7)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"By evaluating the discriminant of the equation x² + 4x + 9 = 0, what can you conclude about its roots?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By evaluating the discriminant of the equation x² + 4x + 9 = 0, what can you conclude about its roots?"}]}]},"e":"b² − 4ac = 16 − 36 = −20. A negative discriminant means the equation has no real roots, so the curve does not meet the x-axis.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"the discriminant","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Δ = b² − 4ac: negative means no real roots, zero means a repeated root, positive means two roots.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["It is negative, so there are no real roots","It is negative, so there are two equal real roots","It is positive, so there are two distinct real roots","It is zero, so the curve touches the x-axis"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"Simplify (2^5 × 2^−3) ÷ 2^4 and state the value of the result.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Simplify (2^5 × 2^−3) ÷ 2^4 and state the value of the result."}]}]},"e":"2^5 × 2^−3 = 2^2, and 2^2 ÷ 2^4 = 2^−2 = 1/4. Adding all three exponents (giving 2^6) treats a division as a multiplication.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"indices","st":"laws of indices","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Multiplying adds exponents, dividing subtracts them, and a negative exponent means a reciprocal.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["1/4","4","64","1/2"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"Solve 5^(2x − 1) = 125 and state the value of x.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solve 5^(2x − 1) = 125 and state the value of x."}]}]},"e":"125 = 5³, so 2x − 1 = 3 and x = 2. Setting 2x − 1 = 125 divides instead of matching exponents.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"solving by matching bases","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Rewrite 125 as a power of 5 first, then equate the exponents and solve the linear equation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["x = 2","x = 63","x = 1","x = 2.5"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"A geometric sequence has u_1 = 5 and r = −2. What is u_6?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence has u_1 = 5 and r = −2. What is u_6?"}]}]},"e":"u_6 = 5 × (−2)^5 = 5 × (−32) = −160. The sign alternates, so an even-indexed term is now negative: u_6 uses an odd power of −2.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"negative common ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A negative ratio alternates signs; (−2)^5 is negative because the power is odd.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["−160","160","−320","−62"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"An infinite geometric series has first term a and common ratio r. Which condition guarantees a finite sum?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An infinite geometric series has first term a and common ratio r. Which condition guarantees a finite sum?"}]}]},"e":"The formula S∞ = a/(1 − r) requires |r| < 1, which is what makes the terms shrink towards zero fast enough for the total to settle.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"limits of series","st":"convergence condition","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Only a ratio smaller than 1 in size makes the successive terms shrink.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["|r| < 1","r > 0","a < r","|r| > 1"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"Three numbers form an arithmetic sequence. The first term is 5 and the third term is 21. What is the middle number?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three numbers form an arithmetic sequence. The first term is 5 and the third term is 21. What is the middle number?"}]}]},"e":"In an arithmetic sequence the middle term is the mean of its neighbours: (5 + 21)/2 = 13, giving d = 8. Halving the difference (8) alone gives the step, not the middle term.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"arithmetic mean","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"With equal steps, the middle term is exactly halfway between the outer two.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["13","8","16","26"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two membership models both start at $100. Model L adds $3 for every visit. Model E multiplies the total by 1.5 for every block of visits completed, so it grows by a constant factor. Which statement is justified?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two membership models both start at "},{"type":"text","text":"$100. Model L adds $"},{"type":"text","text":"3 for every visit. Model E multiplies the total by 1.5 for every block of visits completed, so it grows by a constant factor. Which statement is justified?"}]}]},"e":"A linear model (100 + 3n) grows by a constant amount each visit, while a doubling model grows by a constant factor. The exponential model starts lower but inevitably overtakes the linear one and then grows far faster, so ''linear is always cheaper'' is false.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"comparing linear and exponential growth","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Linear growth adds a constant; exponential growth multiplies by a constant, so it eventually dominates.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","option_labels":["The doubling model eventually overtakes the linear one and then grows far faster","The linear model is always cheaper because it starts lower","Both models grow at the same rate because both start at 100","The doubling model exceeds the linear one after every single visit"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":23,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A theatre has 24 rows of seats. The first row has 18 seats and each row has 2 more seats than the row in front of it.\n\n(a) State the number of seats in the 10th row.\n(b) Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.\n(c) A renovation adds one extra seat to every row. Explain how the total changes.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A theatre has 24 rows of seats. The first row has 18 seats and each row has 2 more seats than the row in front of it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the number of seats in the 10th row.\n(b) Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.\n(c) A renovation adds one extra seat to every row. Explain how the total changes."}]}]},"e":"u_10 = 36, u_24 = 64, so S_24 = 12 × 82 = 984 seats. Adding one seat per row adds 24 seats.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"arithmetic sequences","st":"arithmetic sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"For an arithmetic series find the first and last terms, then apply S_n = n/2 (u_1 + u_n).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the number of seats in the 10th row.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The first row has 18 seats and each row adds 2.","u_n = 18 + 2(n − 1), so use n = 10."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":36,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the number of seats in the last (24th) row.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use n = 24 in the nth-term formula.","18 + 2(23)."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":64,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total number of seats in the theatre.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["S_n = n/2 × (first + last).","24/2 × (18 + 64)."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":984,"tolerance":0,"unit":"seats"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain how the total changes when one seat is added to every row.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["How many rows are there?","Each row gains exactly one seat."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["the total rises by 24 seats, one for each of the 24 rows, giving 1008"],"keywords":[["total"],["rises"],["seats"],["rows"],["giving"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the nth-term formula explicitly in (a); the same formula drives (b) and (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for substituting n = 24, 1 for 64. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 82, 1 for 984. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 24 seats, 1 for 1 008."},{"n":24,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A culture of bacteria doubles every 3 hours. It starts with 500 bacteria.\n\n(a) State the number of bacteria after 12 hours.\n(b) Explain why the number after t hours is not a linear function of t.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete 3-hour periods needed for the population to exceed 50 000.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A culture of bacteria doubles every 3 hours. It starts with 500 bacteria."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the number of bacteria after 12 hours.\n(b) Explain why the number after t hours is not a linear function of t.\n(c) Calculate the number of complete 3-hour periods needed for the population to exceed 50 000."}]}]},"e":"Four periods give 500 × 16 = 8 000 bacteria. The growth is 500 × 2^(t/3), a geometric model. 2^k > 100 first holds at k = 7 (128), so 7 periods are needed.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Doubling is multiplication: count periods, then use a power. Compare with linear growth explicitly.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the number of doubling periods in 12 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The population doubles every 3 hours.","Divide 12 by 3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the number of bacteria after 12 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply the starting 500 by 2 once for each period.","500 × 2^4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":8000,"tolerance":0,"unit":"bacteria"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the population is not a linear function of time.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare what happens in successive 3-hour periods.","Is a constant amount added each period, or a constant factor?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["each period multiplies the population by 2 rather than adding a fixed number, so the growth is geometric (exponential), not linear"],"keywords":[["period"],["multiplies"],["population"],["rather"],["adding"],["fixed"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the number of complete periods needed to exceed 50 000 bacteria.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["You need 500 × 2^k > 50 000, so 2^k > 100.","2^6 = 64 and 2^7 = 128, so take the first k that works."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":7,"tolerance":0,"unit":"periods"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the population against the period number in the workspace: the curve (not a straight line) is the evidence for (c)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^4, 1 for 8 000. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''multiply by 2'', 1 for the linear contrast. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the inequality 2^k > 100, 1 for testing powers, 1 for 7."},{"n":25,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Amara invests $5 000 at 4% per year compound interest. Her friend Ben invests $5 000 at 4% per year simple interest.\n\n(a) Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years.\n(b) Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.\n(c) Explain why the difference grows each year.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Amara invests "},{"type":"text","text":"$5 000 at 4% per year compound interest. Her friend Ben invests $"},{"type":"text","text":"5 000 at 4% per year simple interest."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years.\n(b) Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.\n(c) Explain why the difference grows each year."}]}]},"e":"Amara: 5000(1.04)^5 = $6 083.26. Ben: 5000 + 5(200) = $6 000. The $83.26 gap exists because Amara''s interest compounds, so each year''s interest is calculated on a larger balance.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Compound interest is geometric; simple interest is arithmetic. State the base each year''s interest is calculated on.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the value of Amara''s investment after 5 years, to the nearest cent.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use A = P(1 + r)^n with r = 0.04 and n = 5.","5000 × 1.04^5."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":6083.26,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the value of Ben''s investment after 5 years.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Simple interest adds 4% of $5 000 each year.","5000 + 5 × 200."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":6000,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the gap between the two investments widens each year.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What is each person''s interest calculated on?","Does Ben''s interest ever earn interest?"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["amara earns interest on the interest she has already received, so her balance grows by an increasing amount, while ben always earns 4% of the original $5000"],"keywords":[["amara"],["earns"],["interest"],["already"],["received"],["balance"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap.","marking_criteria":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show P(1 + r)^n with the numbers substituted; the exponent is the point being assessed."},"marking":"(a) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 1.04^5, 1 for $6 083.26. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5 × 200, 1 for $6 000. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''interest on interest'', 1 for the constant simple base, 1 for the widening gap."},{"n":26,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A ball is dropped from 2 m. After each bounce it rises to 60% of its previous height.\n\n(a) State the height after the first bounce.\n(b) Calculate the height after the third bounce.\n(c) Calculate the total vertical distance travelled before the ball comes to rest.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A ball is dropped from 2 m. After each bounce it rises to 60% of its previous height."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the height after the first bounce.\n(b) Calculate the height after the third bounce.\n(c) Calculate the total vertical distance travelled before the ball comes to rest."}]}]},"e":"Bounce heights 1.2, 0.72, 0.432, … have sum 1.2/0.4 = 3 m. The ball travels that sum up and down, so 2 × 3 = 6 m of bounces, plus the initial 2 m fall: 8 m in total.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"limits of series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In a bouncing-ball problem, list the bounce heights as a geometric sequence and remember each bounce is travelled twice.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the height after the first bounce, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["60% of the 2 m drop.","2 × 0.6."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1.2,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"m"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the height after the third bounce, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The heights form a geometric sequence with r = 0.6.","2 × 0.6^3."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":0.432,"tolerance":0.002,"unit":"m"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why the bounce distances are multiplied by 2 in the total distance.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Does the ball only go up, or up and then down?","Each bounce contributes a rise and an equal fall."],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["each bounce is travelled going up and again coming down, so the bounce sum is counted twice, plus the original 2 m drop"],"keywords":[["bounce"],["travelled"],["going"],["again"],["coming"],["down"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the total vertical distance before the ball comes to rest, in metres.","kind":"numeric","points":4,"hints":["Sum the bounce heights with S∞ = a/(1 − r).","Add the original 2 m fall and double the bounce sum."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":8,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"m"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write the bounce heights as a sequence before summing; the doubling in (c) is where most marks are lost."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the ratio, 1 for 0.432. (c) 3 marks: 1 for ''up and down'', 1 for the doubling, 1 for including the first drop. (d) 4 marks: 1 for S∞, 1 for 3 m, 1 for 6 m, 1 for 8 m."},{"n":27,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The 2nd term of a geometric sequence is 12 and the 5th term is 96.\n\n(a) Explain how to find the common ratio from these two terms.\n(b) Calculate the common ratio.\n(c) Calculate the first term.\n(d) State the 7th term.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 2nd term of a geometric sequence is 12 and the 5th term is 96."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain how to find the common ratio from these two terms.\n(b) Calculate the common ratio.\n(c) Calculate the first term.\n(d) State the 7th term."}]}]},"e":"u_5/u_2 = r³ = 8 gives r = 2, so u_1 = 12/2 = 6 and u_7 = 6 × 2^6 = 384.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"geometric sequences","st":"geometric sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Dividing two terms of a geometric sequence gives r raised to the difference in their positions.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Explain how the two terms give the common ratio.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Write u_2 and u_5 in terms of u_1 and r.","How many factors of r separate the 2nd and 5th terms?"],"strand":"C(i)","accepted":["dividing u_5 by u_2 removes the first term and leaves r^3, because the terms are three positions apart"],"keywords":[["dividing"],["removes"],["first"],["term"],["leaves"],["terms"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the common ratio.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["96 ÷ 12 = 8, and that equals r³.","Take the cube root of 8."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":2,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the first term.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["u_2 = u_1 × r.","12 = 2 × u_1."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":6,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the 7th term.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The 7th term is six multiplications of r after the first term.","u_7 = 6 × 2^6."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":384,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (a) use the word ''divide'' and the difference in position (3 steps); that is the reasoning being marked."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for ''three positions, so r cubed''. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 8, 1 for r = 2. (c) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 6. (d) 2 marks: 1 for r^6, 1 for 384."},{"n":28,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A savings plan pays $200 in month 1, $220 in month 2, $240 in month 3, and so on, increasing by $20 each month.\n\n(a) State the amount paid in month 12.\n(b) Calculate the total saved after 12 months.\n(c) Explain how many months are needed for the total to exceed $5 000, setting up the inequality you would solve.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A savings plan pays "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 in month 1, $"},{"type":"text","text":"220 in month 2, "},{"type":"text","text":"$240 in month 3, and so on, increasing by $"},{"type":"text","text":"20 each month."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the amount paid in month 12.\n(b) Calculate the total saved after 12 months.\n(c) Explain how many months are needed for the total to exceed "},{"type":"text","text":"$5 000, setting up the inequality you would solve."}]}]},"e":"Month 12 is 200 + 11(20) = $420 and S_12 = 6(620) = $3 720. S_14 = 7(200 + 460) = $4 620, which is still below 5000, while S_15 = 7.5(200 + 480) = $5 100 exceeds it, so 15 months are needed.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"sigma notation","st":"sigma notation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For ''when does the total exceed X'', compute running sums and bracket the answer between two months rather than guessing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the amount paid in month 12, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The amounts form an arithmetic sequence with d = 20.","200 + 11 × 20."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":420,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total saved after 12 months, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["S_n = n/2 × (first + last).","12/2 × (200 + 420)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":3720,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the total saved after 14 months, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use the formula with n = 14 and last term 200 + 13(20).","14/2 × (200 + 460)."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":4620,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the first month for which the total exceeds $5 000.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Compare your 14-month total with 5000; it is still short.","Add one more month''s payment to your 14-month total and compare again."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"months"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Build the monthly totals in the workspace table; the sequence of running totals answers (d) directly."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for the last term, 1 for $3 720. (c) 3 marks: 1 for n = 14, 1 for the last term 460, 1 for $4 620. (d) 2 marks: 1 for comparing, 1 for 15 months."},{"n":29,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The number of bacteria in a dish is modelled by N = 300 × 2^t, where t is in hours.\n\n(a) State the initial number of bacteria.\n(b) Calculate the number of bacteria after 5 hours.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the time for the population to reach 20 000, to 2 decimal places.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The number of bacteria in a dish is modelled by N = 300 × 2^t, where t is in hours."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the initial number of bacteria.\n(b) Calculate the number of bacteria after 5 hours.\n(c) Using logarithms, calculate the time for the population to reach 20 000, to 2 decimal places."}]}]},"e":"N(0) = 300 and N(5) = 9 600. For 20 000, 2^t = 66.67 so t = log 66.67 / log 2 ≈ 6.06 hours.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"exponential equations","st":"exponential equations","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"To solve for an exponent, isolate the power and take logarithms of both sides.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the initial number of bacteria.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Substitute t = 0.","2^0 = 1."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":300,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the number of bacteria after 5 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute t = 5.","300 × 32."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":9600,"tolerance":0,"unit":"bacteria"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the value of 20 000 ÷ 300, to 3 significant figures.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide the target by the initial population.","This equals 2^t."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":66.7,"tolerance":0.1},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the time to reach 20 000 bacteria, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Take logarithms of both sides: t = log(66.67) ÷ log(2).","Your answer should be a little more than 6 hours."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":6.06,"tolerance":0.03,"unit":"hours"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^5, 1 for 9 600. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 66.7. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logarithms, 1 for 6.06.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^5, 1 for 9 600. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 66.7. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logarithms, 1 for 6.06.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the logarithm step in (d); the division only sets up the equation."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 2^5, 1 for 9 600. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 66.7. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logarithms, 1 for 6.06."}]'::jsonb) as x
